Z||000000||88formula||07-05-2001||10:33 PM||||Does anyone actually know the fiero’s center of gravity in inches? I've heard it was 19" but not sure. How would this compare too newer sports cars?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||07-05-2001||10:44 PM||||I think its about 24 inches<P>  below the surface of the street (c:<P>  the way these things corner they remind me of the slot cars I had when I was a kid.||||reg||11
Z||000002||theogre||07-05-2001||10:48 PM||||Actually, you want to know 2 things. the CG and the roll center. Most people don't know that the roll center and CG are not the same thing.<P>CG is always above the pavement, but if I remember correctly the roll center of a really stable car is usually below the pavement. I would like to find both numbers but I doubt that the roll center is public.<P>Also, roll center is affected by tires and other things more than CG.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||07-05-2001||10:58 PM||||Gee thanks Org. How do you know so much about these cars? You’re like a walking fiero encyclopedia man. I don’t know much about roll center and center of gravity or how they affect vehicle handling other than the basics.  Maybe you could enlighten me a little about CG and roll center. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-05-2001||11:14 PM||||CG is simply the balance point of the car. In theory, if you stuck a pivot there you can rotate the car in any direction, stop, and it stays wher you put it.<P>Roll center is the point the car actually rotates on in a turn etc. This point, for any decent handling car should be below the pavement. As compared to a Winnebago where it's pobably 6 feet above ground. (Kidding, but you get the idea of how a big camper handles..... It don't.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||07-05-2001||11:20 PM||||Center of gravity of any object is the point in the object where there exists an equal amount of mass on each and every side of it.  CG can also be referred to as the center of mass.  If you could balance a Fiero on a stick or something, the point at which it balanced is the CM or CG.  Those two terms are used interchangeably because gravity acts on mass.<P>If you were to hang a Fiero by a bunch of different points, and drew a line straight down from each point towards the ground while it was hung, where all your "hanging point lines" met is the CG.<P>Roll center is more of a suspension thing.  I do not have a very good explanation of roll center, but my uncle, who is a senior GM engineer, said that the roll center of Fieros is very close to the physical center of the car.  I guess you could define physical center as the midpoint of the wheel base and then the midpoint of the track of the car.<P>I can only speculate that the roll center has something to do with which point of the car moves the least during body roll?  hell I dont know, I'm listening too ogre!!<P>edit:  Well damn!  I guess I was kinda right on that!  I posted too late, theogre beat me to it (as usual  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||07-06-2001||10:03 AM||||I've heard the CG is 19.5 inches off the ground.<P>The roll center of a car is the imaginary axis about which the car rolls as it corners. A car's roll center is determined entirely by suspension geometry and not affected at all by tires, spring rates, CG height, etc.<P>The best compromise for roll center height is between ground level and 3" above ground level.||||reg||1
